# Fighting for Purpose

This is a rough season for me. The things I’ve historically depended on for my “joie de vivre”—my feelings of purpose, belonging, drive—have all been collapsing, or have collapsed. Without these things, life loses its flavor, becomes bland, and it becomes hard to feel the point in anything.

The antidote to depression has never been “happiness”—it’s hope. Hope that things can/will get better. I’ve lost quite a lot of my hope in some things I’ve held dear, that have helped give me purpose. It is, therefore, necessary for me to find new things (or to revisit old, laid-aside things) to fill the gap. There is some small progress, there. But there are also some things that will prove very difficult to replace.

## The pillars I’ve lost

Either completely, or large chunks.

### National identity

It’s been well over a decade since I felt any love for my country. But I’ve been comfortable enough being an American, despite our tendency to narcissistic bravado, and general blindness to our own flaws. I’d have been surprised if you told me that I depend on my comfort with living in America, being an American, as one of the things I depend on for my emotional well-being—it’s been quite a while since I had much of that.

But now, what was once a general lack of comfort is now excruciating torment. It is an agony to live in America, as an American citizen, and live every day with the continually-unfolding consequences of having elected a hateful and cartoonishly-evil administration, all while tens of millions enthusiastically cheer. And it doesn’t matter if we “fix things”, if everything returns to a degree of normalcy after some future election. The fact that this was even remotely possible, let alone was voted in—twice!—has turned my distrust into disgusted contempt for the American populace.

Are there plenty of other Americans who feel the same disgust and rage at what’s being done in our name, as I do? Yes, of course. But there are far, far too many who celebrate the hate, and far too many others more than willing to excuse it, or to insist that we continue civil discourse with people who’ve repeatedly demonstrated they’ve no interest in acting in good faith.

The obvious answer is to move. Perhaps I’ll write a later post diving into the details, but for now: believe me, I want to—but it’s simply not possible for me, at least not for my current situation. I’m desperately working to change that, but it’s going to be a long process, and may not really be feasible.

Added to that, is the concerning rise of nationalism, racism, and authoritarianism across the globe. America may be the latest one to reach the point of no return (joinning such countries as Russia and China), but many of the other ones I’d be interested in moving to, also show worrying signs that they’re moving in the same general direction (albeit at a slower pace, for the moment anyway).

Even so, better to live in a place that’s merely threatening to fall off a cliff, than to continue residing where we’re already in freefall.

### Career

A lot of fulfillment and contentment in my life has come from the thrill of problem-solving that comes with my career as a Software Engineer. But now we’ve automated away all the bits of software development that keep my career stimulating/rewarding/vitalizing. Vibe-coding has robbed me of my job satisfaction, and of the prospect of having it basically anywhere in this career field. At least until the current levels of hype and overwrought zeal calm, at any rate.

It’s also quite difficult to focus on being productive when virtually every day there’s some new horror, some murder committed and justified in the name of the government, some travesty against democracy or the checks-and-balances so fundamental to healthy government, some apalling statement of hate.

### Personal projects

A lot of the coding I do isn’t for work, but for my personal pleasure. A lot of it, for vintage computing platforms, especially the Apple II. But a succession of experiences have damaged my sense of community in retro computing.

I code for my pleasure, not for the approval of others, and not to form communities. Even so, having _found_ a community around the things I love to use and create, and subsequently losing my zest for it, has nonetheless damaged my own zeal for continuing to use and create hobbyist software.

### Family

Being the father of my three kids is something that’s brought me a tremendous amount of personal satisfaction.

But, as discussed in another previous post, I cut contact with one of my kids, my oldest son, after he began directly threatening my life, with rather visceral detail, and I’ve recently learned my relationship with my daughter, long under strain, has been even more damaged than I had realized, due to misinterpretations too long ago and deep-seated to effectively repair now.

Watching my son fall to psychosis and hatred, and realizing there will never be any real closeness with my daughter, has ripped a fair bit of joy from my life, already struggling in the face of living in a nation that’s gleefully hell-bent on destroying freedom and empathy.

## Pillars that remain

Here are a few of the things still in my life—still propping me up.

### Family and Friends

Despite the ragged holes in my heart, in the shapes of my oldest two kids, I’m very grateful for the relationships that remain. With my wife, with my youngest son (19). With my parents and siblings.

I tend to be particular with my friendships, so am never really cultivating more than a couple. I am very grateful for the friends I have, and for the strength of those friendships.

### Community

Right now there’s really only one community I feel genuinely energized by—our local meetup chapter of Recovering from Religion. It’s both healing, and energizing, to have conversations and share experiences with people who also grew up indoctrinated, under religious control.

### Music

Making music with others is one of the most reliable and healing things I am doing, and I need to lean into that more.

### Music: Improvised Music Jams

There’s a semi-local meetup group for just getting together and jamming, musically. They have a dedicated, quality studio, with mics, inputs, monitors, and instruments, including guitars, strings, a trumpet, two keyboards, and an accoustic baby grand. They use a bespoke web application to show chord sheets for whatever song we’re playing, with displays around the room for convenience.

I generally try to go on Mondays, and on any Saturdays where I have the time and energy. On Saturdays I tend to play accordion (a C-system chromatic button accordion), as I can use the practice to improvise on it. On Mondays, and the occasional Thursday, I’ll play piano (where I’m far more facile), and occasionally change it up with my accordina

...What’s an “accordina”? See the below link of Ludovic Beier masterfully playing his. It's something of a cross between a chromatic button accordion, a harmonica, and a melodica. Its keyboard is effectively the right-hand/treble side of a chromatic button accordion, but like the other two instruments it is powered by the player’s breath, rather than a bellows. The sound it makes is close to a harmonica... but like a melodica it uses a keyboard (but not like a piano’s), and you can form just about any kind of chord or polyphony with a lot more ease than you can with a harmonica.

As far as I know, there are just a small few artisans in France that make these. Mine was made by Joseph Carrel.

=> https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=him8HWZmTNs&list=PLdOcEkhWdHTdbKcJqqfc45OEFUJyBkXbA&index=15 Ludovic Beier, Accordina Mon Amour

When I was an Evangelical Christian, I was heavily involved in worship ministry. It was a huge part of my life. When I outgrew my faith, I stopped having an outlet for playing improvised music, and it’s been genuinely healing, and uplifting, for me to be playing to chord sheets with other musicians. I didn’t realize just how much I missed that, needed that.

### Music: Piano Accompaniment

I’ve been involved, the past few years, with accompanying high school instrumentalists as a pianist. Solo instrumentalists doing local competition events, and a couple of high school musical theatre productions. Recognizing that music needs to be a bigger part of my life these days, to shore up some of these lost pillars of emotional fortitude, I have just accepted a (very) part-time position as accompanist for the three choirs at a local middle school.

This kind of playing involves a very different set of musical skills. Playing from sheet music (rather than chords), and requiring greater general focus in order to complement the other musician(s) (but requiring far less in the way of improvisation or creativity). It is satisfying and rewarding in largely different ways than the casual jam sessions. It also has me working with youth, which is the segment of humanity I can still have the most hope in.

### Music: Other Things I Should Be Doing

Given how much potential for emotional healing and strength that music has in my life, I want to seek to do much more. Play at open mics. Compose songs. Compose instrumental piano music.

Arrange things for accordion (I enjoy a well-played accordion, but I’m not too keen on the traditional American and Western European accordion repertoire (tired polkas and marches with all their oompa!-oompa! can pretty much go fuck themselves). Eastern Europe tends to take the accordion/bayan much more seriously, and the repertoire reflects this. This leaves a lot of opportunity to compose or arrange things for accordion that don’t actually sound terrible.
